From: Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com>
Subject: [PATCH] AzCore/Script: drop redundant <Lua/lobject.h> include

`Code/Framework/AzCore/AzCore/Script/ScriptContext.cpp` includes the
internal Lua header `<Lua/lobject.h>` solely to obtain the
`LUAI_MAXALIGN` macro, which is then used to define a backward-compat
`union L_Umaxalign` (consumed in `sizeof()` expressions at lines
2781, 2783, 4502, 4504 for alignment-padded userdata sizing).

`LUAI_MAXALIGN` is documented public Lua API:
- Defined in luaconf.h (Lua's public configuration header) — the @@
  doc-comment marker is Lua's convention for public config macros
- Used in lauxlib.h's public `luaL_Buffer` struct definition

The existing `<Lua/lualib.h>` and `<Lua/lauxlib.h>` includes in the
same `extern "C"` block already pull luaconf.h transitively
(lualib.h -> lua.h -> luaconf.h, and lauxlib.h -> luaconf.h directly),
so `LUAI_MAXALIGN` is in scope without `<Lua/lobject.h>`.

Verified empirically against Fedora 44's lua-devel-5.4.8: a minimal
test program with only `<lua.h>` + `<lualib.h>` + `<lauxlib.h>` (no
lobject.h) compiles, links, and runs `sizeof(L_Umaxalign)` to a
real positive integer.

Side benefit: removing the internal-header dependency reduces
brittleness against future Lua internal-API reorganization, and
enables o3de to build against system Lua on Linux distributions
that ship Lua public-API-only (Fedora's lua-devel, Debian's
liblua5.4-dev, Alpine's lua5.4-dev, etc.) — historically these
distros have been blocked from packaging o3de because their
lua-devel doesn't include lobject.h.

Behavior-preserving: same union, same sizeof(), same alignment
math; only the redundant header include is dropped. Comment
updated to document the public-API path so the next reader
doesn't reach for lobject.h again.

Signed-off-by: Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com>
---
--- a/Code/Framework/AzCore/AzCore/Script/ScriptContext.cpp
+++ b/Code/Framework/AzCore/AzCore/Script/ScriptContext.cpp
@@ -25,11 +25,10 @@
 extern "C" {
 #   include <Lua/lualib.h>
 #   include <Lua/lauxlib.h>
-#   include <Lua/lobject.h>
 
-    // versions of LUA before 5.3.x used to define a union that contained a double, a pointer, and a long
-    // as L_Umaxalign.  Newer versions define those inner types in the macro LUAI_MAXALIGN instead but
-    // no longer actually declare a union around it.  For backward compatibility we define the same one here
+    // Lua < 5.3.x defined a union containing double + pointer + long as L_Umaxalign. Lua 5.3+ replaced the
+    // union with the LUAI_MAXALIGN macro (defined in luaconf.h, public API; also used in lauxlib.h's
+    // luaL_Buffer). Wrap it in a union here so existing call sites can keep using sizeof(L_Umaxalign).
     union L_Umaxalign { LUAI_MAXALIGN; };
 }
 
